{"output": "Mei turned off the greenhouse lights to their night cycle — eighteen hours had passed since dawn, and the plants needed the dark period for their circadian rhythms to function correctly — and the module dimmed to a deep, warm amber. The far-red LEDs pulsed once, twice, telling the plants that the day was ending. She stood in the amber dark and felt the familiar exhaustion that came from the kind of work that used the whole body: the mental load of tracking a dozen variables simultaneously, the physical labor of mixing soil and water and nutrients, the emotional toll of caring for living things in a place that had no intention of sustaining them.

"Good work today," she said to the dark. Not to the AI, she realized after a moment, even though the AI was listening. To the plants. To the greenhouse itself, as a space, as a vessel, as the only place on this planet where the chemistry of life proceeded with the same inevitability it had on Earth.

*End of Chapter 21 — Bridge: The greens are hope, but calories are what the colony actually needs.*", "exit_code": 0, "error": null}
